# Raw secret/vulnerability scan output. NEVER commit — a gitleaks report embeds the MATCHED
# SECRET VALUE inline for every finding, so committing one publishes exactly what the scan
# was run to find, and pushing it means those credentials must be treated as compromised
# regardless of any later deletion.
#
# Prompted by gitleaks-report.json + gitleaks-report-redacted.json appearing untracked in the
# working tree. Verified at the time this was added: no scan report has EVER been committed on
# any branch (`git log --all --diff-filter=A`), so this is preventive, not remedial — there is
# no history to purge.
#
# gitleaks is run AD HOC (it is not in CI or package.json), which is precisely why its output
# lands loose in the repo root where a stray `git add -A` would take it. The other tools below
# are not wired here either; they are listed because a secret/dependency sweep is the kind of
# thing that gets run by hand next to gitleaks, and the cost of covering them is nil.
#
# Deliberately NOT a blanket `*-report.json`: that would silently swallow a legitimate future
# artifact. Name the tools; add one when a new tool is actually used.
gitleaks-report*.json
gitleaks-report*.sarif
semgrep-report*.json
semgrep.sarif
trivy-report*.json
snyk-report*.json
npm-audit*.json
